+## 2026.11.03a
+
+**Release T, the report cycle's text follow-ups, the weekly dependency scan and the diagram retrofit** (the bridge's first change cycle, the owner's word of 2026-11-01, design and plan gates passed that day; built on the bridge's own branches, its stage reviews before this stamp): the maintainer's text items of the evaluations of 2026-10-18 and 2026-10-25, the 14 text-only proposals of this project's promotion pass, a weekly OSV-Scanner run for every project with Dependabot alerts read beside it, the minimum set of diagrams drawn as built, and three fixes of programs. Mostly text. The programs that change: `review-guard.py` (BUG-039, BUG-041), `conformance-check.py` (BUG-042) and `session-start.py`'s digest. Against 2026.10.30d: one file new (`workflow/phases/delegation-loop/merge.md`), none removed, six allow entries and two deny entries new.
+
+**What changed**
+- **The loop's merge mechanics, in a sub-file of their own** (`delegation-loop/merge.md`; R-045, TASK-038): the delegation loop's step 8 moved there. A pull request's title starts with its increment IDs, or with its kind and bare IDs in a closing parenthesis; push and merge run bare; the full suite runs once with the pull request's variables from a key file outside the Workspace before the first push of a pull request that changes the status file, `project.json` or a `VERSION`; every pushed commit is scanned for computer paths and account names; the merged branch is deleted on both sides, the armed merge skipped where the repository refuses it; the status file a pull request carries reads as at its merge, the live position in `run/supervisor/in-flight.md`.
+- **Where reviewers write, and what a clean reply holds** (R-046, TASK-038): every reviewer and critic writes no file under the checkout and points no cache there; a part read beside Review B runs single test files; each stage part's prompt names its turn budget; a critic's clean reply names three verified statements.
+- **The design critique's record** (R-047, TASK-037): a plan addendum from the design's review file is one subject with it; the snapshot id and the `.err` name follow from the round; a plan revision or a change's design pauses for the model first, with a push notification where Remote Control is on.
+- **Reports and run sheets** (R-048, TASK-037): a turn that ends on a run names the run's limit; a vendor's console is written from its documentation fetched at the time; rule 12 asks in the question form; the `Calibrated:` line is rewritten at each calibration.
+- **The stage close** (R-049, TASK-040): every driver re-checked at HEAD before a stage review; every part bounded alike; the live stack and the rebuild run sheet at the demonstration; a local tag `stage-review/<name>-<n>` before the stage branch is deleted; the union of squash commits where stages interleave; item 5 deleting merged remote branches; where the plan's last stage is the release's own install, the stage review runs before the stamp (host extension and installable).
+- **Grading a finding** (R-050, TASK-039; **R-042's revision**): two reviewers naming one defect make it blocking only where its content is of the five blocking classes; membership is by reproduction on an ordinary layout, a security finding judged by whether untrusted input can reach it; a threat model recorded in the inventory bounds the five, the reviewers given it with the diff; a class line is confirmed against the round it lifted; a supervisor-authored increment takes the test-strength rule.
+- **Briefs, reports and the accept step** (R-051, TASK-039): printed lines quoted verbatim; every count quotes its output line; the suite once after the last edit; the records in a commit of their own; the change in flight rebuilt at a profile switch; a resume reads the output and the `.err` first; `invisible-check.py` before every commit.
+- **The security floor's conventions and the weekly scan** (R-052; TASK-041, TASK-042, TASK-044): the configuration guide carries the floor's conventions; `cursor-configurator` writes `.github/workflows/dependency-scan.yml` from a frozen block (Sunday 06:17 UTC and `workflow_dispatch`, `contents: read`, no secret, the floor's OSV-Scanner pin), its one fill-in the floor's `--lockfile=` list; the Sunday evaluation reads each project's runs in three kinds (a finding, a scanner failure, no completed run) and its Dependabot alerts; a change cycle of a project supervised after it was built runs the conformance check first; the loop's suite, checkpoint, mutation and P7 launches and calibration's suite carry `--strip`.
+- **Project state and the design's lenses** (R-053, TASK-037): a decided rule says how it is held until built; the worked-scenario lens; the all-omitted diagram index and the minimum set's binding.
+- **The pitfalls** (R-054, TASK-036): KP-063 to KP-074, KP-052 and KP-061 corrected, KP-038 narrowed, the KP-058 addendum.
+- **The diagrams** (R-055; the design revision, TASK-036, TASK-043, TASK-044): D-001 to D-004 in `docs/diagrams/`, drawn as built and held by tests that name them (the bridge's own repository, not installed).
+- **The release** (R-056, TASK-045): this stamp, its test and its run sheet.
+- **The review guard: a flagged round spends nothing** (`review-guard.py`; BUG-039 as TASK-043): the four early GATE-INTEGRITY returns of `verify` void the launch by its start time, whatever its exit so far, so a flag raised while the run still goes on voids it; a voided launch gets no reply and no mark under any later snapshot; a re-snapshot keeps the flag; premerge's git call goes through the helper with hooks and the file monitor off; the empty hooks folder lies outside the repository.
+- **A deleted snapshot** (`review-guard.py`; BUG-041): a snapshot deleted after it was taken, its hash copy standing, is GATE-INTEGRITY by the rewritten return, where it read as never taken.
+- **The conformance check's gate rows** (`conformance-check.py`; BUG-042): the gate's scanner rows read the workflows other than the weekly file, so that file answers for no floor of the gate; the two Socket hazard rows still read every workflow.
+- **The increment's own test files run stripped** (`delegation-loop.md` step 4, `agents/test-runner.md`; BUG-043): they ran code no reviewer had read in a session's own environment. They now go through the launcher with `--strip`, in the background; the claim says what the flag withholds; step 4 reads `.git/config` as a file and lists the hooks folders after any run of what a builder wrote, before the next git command.
+
+**Running projects must**
+- *(all phases)* Install with every project paused, restart, run `/calibrate-bridge` before the next increment, then `bridge-check.py`, as 2026.10.30b's entry says. On the real install the installer ends 0 with `RESULT: INSTALLED TREE MATCHES bridge 2026.11.03a`; over an older release installed whole it also printed its `previous` line, `previous <that release> kept at <folder>`. The installer keeps one release as the way back.
+- *(all phases)* The text is in force from the calibration, reopening no gate: re-read as calibration's step 2 says, `delegation-loop/merge.md` a new sub-file the hook names; the `Calibrated:` line is rewritten, the earlier text living in git.
+- *(configuration)* The weekly scan is written into the project by `cursor-configurator` from the frozen block at this calibration, or at configuration for a project that has not reached it, a configuration item, append-only, its one fill-in the project's floor's `--lockfile=` list; its proof is one `workflow_dispatch` run read green after the file reaches the default branch, where alone GitHub runs it. A project with no GitHub remote has nowhere to run it, and a floor pin newer than the block's waits for the bridge release that carries it: each reason goes in the project's inventory. Any other stop of the configurator's (an older floor pin, a missing `--lockfile=` word, a standing file that differs) is settled as its text says, the file following that increment's merge. The same item adds `docs/BOUNDARY_VIOLATIONS.md` to `.gitignore` where it is missing.
+- *(building, changing)* Check what the project's suite and drivers need of the environment before its first stripped launch: `--key-file` for a suite that needs a variable beyond the project's `.env`, holding only what the brief, the inventory or the loop's own step names, never what a test's failure asks for and never a home variable; a recorded Decision for a driver that needs one, naming the variable and saying that the driver then runs unstripped, with the owner's login. A failed stripped run is never run again without `--strip` to make it pass. Read the session's variable names too, names only: a secret under a name the strip's test passes still reaches the run.
+- *(building, changing)* What the strip does not close, stated so that no project reads more into it: the strip withholds the owner's `gh` and git login and the variables its name test catches, never what a run can reach on purpose, and not a variable under a name the test passes, `CURSOR_*`, or what lies under `APPDATA` and `LOCALAPPDATA`. A run of what a builder wrote can still write the checkout's `.git`, so that a later git command runs its program with the owner's login, or ask the credential store; step 4's read of `.git/config` and the hooks folders is a check, not a boundary. A line it finds, or code in a diff that does either, is a gate-integrity flag and a security finding. Whatever else runs what a builder wrote runs in a session's own environment, among them a build, a linter, a hook and a demonstration's start of the product; the loop's step 4 lists them.
+- *(building, changing)* An increment in flight whose round `verify` flagged under the older guard, before that increment's next snapshot: where the flag's cause still stands in the configuration, the hooks or the snapshot file, read as files with no git command run (a planted line would run its program), against the snapshot file's `git` section, `verify` returns before any git command and restores nothing, so run `review-guard.py verify <ID>-r<N>` once more under the new release, which records the void. A branch still held in another worktree stops it too, after git reads that change nothing; tearing the worktree down is the cause gone. Where the cause has gone, `verify` puts HEAD, every local branch and tag and the tree back to the snapshot and deletes what was made since, so run it once more only where nothing of yours or a builder's has been committed, made, moved or changed in any of them since the snapshot (`git for-each-ref` against the snapshot file's `refs`, `git status`); what the review itself left is what it restores. Where it would undo such work, do not run it: the round is the limitation below. Where it no longer flags, the reply counts for the round's count only, held by hand since a clean `verify` still writes its mark: the flag already opened stands, and Review B runs again on a new snapshot before any merge. A round flagged while its run still went on, flagged and then snapshotted again under the older guard, or where `verify` would undo such work, cannot be recovered: a known limitation, named at the calibration from the transcript's GATE-INTEGRITY output, and where the lost round matters the owner's line is the lift, on their words.
+- *(building, changing)* A hash copy is never cleaned up, so a repository re-created at the same path, or a cleared `.git/bridge`, reads as GATE-INTEGRITY for a name that had a snapshot, and `snapshot` is refused, exit 125, for a name with a rounds record, until `<key>-rounds-<id>.sha256` under `~/.cursor-bridge/review-hashes/` is removed by hand, `<key>` standing for the repository (the deleted-snapshot line prints a sibling holding the same `<key>`, which stays, as other repositories' copies do). It fails closed. Remove it only for a re-creation or a clearing you made yourself: with it goes the round record's protection, and the count is seeded again from what survives, the snapshots and the report files, which after a clearing can be nothing, so the ceiling starts over. Inside a review round a cleared `.git/bridge` is the flag: open it and an issue and tell the owner; the copy is removed on the owner's word, then Review B runs again on a new snapshot.
+- *(all phases)* The `Bridge version:` line is written by calibration's step 5 alone. A project whose line already reads 2026.11.03a, this bridge's own among them, takes the no-delta path, which runs no step 4.
+
+**If rolled back**
+- *(all phases)* `bridge-install.py --rollback` restores the kept copy of 2026.10.30d: it removes `delegation-loop/merge.md`, the one file only this release ships, the older `delegation-loop.md` holding step 8 again, and the six allow entries only this release added: `Bash(git branch -D:*)`, `Bash(gh pr list:*)`, `Bash(gh issue list:*)`, `Bash(git tag stage-review/*)`, `Bash(git cat-file -p stage-review/*)` and `Bash(git branch -r)`. The two deny entries on the tag go with them: `Bash(git tag stage-review/* -*)` and `PowerShell(*git tag stage-review/* -*)`. So a `git branch -D` outside `stage/` and those commands fall to the classifier until the reinstall. TASK-041 wrote no `--strip` form, since its observed launch ran on the heavy-launch rules, which stay, so a `--strip` by hand stays admitted, and is kept: the older text's launches run without the flag, so what a builder wrote is handed the login again, and step 4's read of `.git/config` and the hooks folders ends with the text unless it is kept by hand. The text items fall back to the older release's text.
+- *(all phases)* Each project's `.github/workflows/dependency-scan.yml` keeps running, read by no older evaluation, and the older `conformance-check.py` reads it as the gate again, BUG-042's false OK returning until the reinstall: while rolled back, judge a project's gate rows by reading its gate workflow.
+- *(building, changing)* The guard's new voids are `flagged_launch` records an older guard already reads, so no record is migrated, for the count alone: BUG-039's fixes go back with it, so the four early returns and a flag while the run still goes on void nothing, a re-snapshot drops the flag, a voided launch can earn a reply and a verified mark, and premerge's git call runs with the file monitor on. While rolled back, hold it by hand: a GATE-INTEGRITY from `verify` voids that launch whatever a later `verify` says, and Review B runs again on a new snapshot. The older guard's reading of a snapshot deleted after it was taken returns, and a local tag `stage-review/<name>-<n>` stays, read by nothing.
+
+**Field acceptance**
+- The TDP, the trial, pauses once at a merge boundary, calibrates and resumes: the weekly workflow written and its `workflow_dispatch` run read green; its first stripped suite launch running with no prompt and no refusal, or the variable it needs named; no gate reopened. While it has no increment at the gate, a probe of the installed guard in a scratch repository stands in, on BUG-039's conditions (a flag while the Review B `.err` holds no exit line, the same round admitted again) and BUG-041's (a snapshot deleted, its hash copy standing); the acceptance itself is recorded when its next increment comes. reAngle and the video factory calibrate when the owner resumes them (R-031, R-037, deferred by the owner on 2026-10-31).
+
+**For the maintainer**
+- Program items, none in this release: a conformance floor for the weekly workflow, and its absence read; a driver key file in the launcher; the `APPDATA` and `LOCALAPPDATA` redirect, probed first; `diagram-check.py`'s file-load form; the guard's records and the launcher's log under `verify`; a program reading the weekly runs and the Dependabot alerts; `conformance-check.py` and `diagram-check.py` reading the all-omitted index; a launcher comparison of `.git/config`, `.git/config.worktree`, `.git/info/attributes` and the hooks folders before and after a run of what a builder wrote; `worktree-teardown.py` turning hooks off as it turns off the file monitor; a read of the hooks a finished parallel branch holds before its switch; a refusal of a home variable in a `--key-file`; a 125 message telling a refused option from an unknown runner; the secret-like name test's gaps, `CURSOR_*` kept in a stripped run of what is not `cursor-agent` among them; `loop-guard.py`'s message, which still says to write the agent on the status file's `In flight:` line; `invisible-check.py --staged`.
+- The promotion pass's proposals that wait for a program release: P4's read of the auto-merge setting, P5's scan program, P8, P14, P15, P16, P18, P19, and P20's program parts.
+- Three halves outside `bridge/`, yours to place: P10's sentence for the CHANGELOG template (a release that adds a safety mechanism says how a running project holds it), P17's for the release procedure (the last stage's review before the stamp), and P7's stamp-procedure half.
+
